(begin-tx "DPDC-G10 — AQP-BOOT's step-shape guards, and the half of each that is MUTE")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 150000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== DPDC-G10 — AQP-BOOT's step-shape guards, and the half of each that is MUTE · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== 
(print "--- [DPDC-G10 — AQP-BOOT's step-shape guards, and the half of each that is MUTE · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
(print "--- [DPDC-G10 · 01 · the six step-shape messages] ---")
;;SIX unpinned guards in 2_CITIZEN/5_VaultsMinter/04_AQP-BOOT.pact, all of them input-shape checks on
;;the boot sequence's step functions. They are here rather than in an AQP harness for one reason: this
;;file already deploys Stage 02 and runs in ~26s, while the AQP harness takes ~62s, and these guards
;;need nothing from either fixture -- AQP-BOOT's steps carry no `P|UEV_IMC`, so they are directly
;;callable and every rejection below fires before any real work happens.
;;
;;The messages are unusually good: each one spells out the exact list the operator was supposed to
;;pass. That is the point of pinning them -- they are operator documentation delivered at the moment
;;of the mistake, and they are only worth having if they actually arrive.
;;==== DPDC-G10 — AQP-BOOT's step-shape guards, and the half of each that is MUTE · 02 · let / invocation ==== 
(print "--- [DPDC-G10 — AQP-BOOT's step-shape guards, and the half of each that is MUTE · 02 · let / invocation] ---")
(let
    (
        (p:string KST.ANHD)
        (x:string "X")
        (a6:[string] ["a" "b" "c" "d" "e" "f"])
        (a9:[string] ["1" "2" "3" "4" "5" "6" "7" "8" "9"])
    )
    (map print
        [
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G10>> step 6 names the three boost classes it wants"
                "Step 6 expects boost-class-ids=[silver bronze golden]."
                (AQP-BOOT.C_Step6_CreateOuroLpTriplet p p x ["a" "b" "c" "d"]))
            ;;step 7 carries FOUR separate length checks, one per list. Each is isolated by keeping the
            ;;other three at their correct lengths -- otherwise the first enforce answers every time
            ;;and three of the four messages would never be exercised.
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G10>> step 7 names the six DH assets"
                "Step 7 expects dh-asset-ids=[coding bloodshed company wondercoach nosferatu bunnies]."
                (AQP-BOOT.C_Step7_CreatePoolsAndScores p (+ a6 ["g"]) x a9 ["s" "b" "g"]))
            ;;REMOVED 2026-09-18. `dh-pool-ids` is no longer an argument: Step 7 mints those
            ;;seven pools itself, from the same name literals its C_Issue calls use, so it now
            ;;derives their ids in its own `let`. There is no length guard left to probe, because
            ;;there is no list left for a caller to get wrong.
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G10>> ...the nine DH scores"
                "Step 7 expects dh-score-ids=[coding sub-coding bloodshed sub-bloodshed company-share company-snakes sub-wondercoach sub-nosferatu sub-bunnies]."
                (AQP-BOOT.C_Step7_CreatePoolsAndScores p a6 x (+ a9 ["10"]) ["s" "b" "g"]))
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G10>> ...and the three OURO triplet scores"
                "Step 7 expects ouro-triplet-score-ids=[silver bronze golden]."
                (AQP-BOOT.C_Step7_CreatePoolsAndScores p a6 x a9 ["s" "b" "g" "h"]))
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G10>> step 9 wants exactly five subsidiary scores"
                "Step 9 expects subsidiary-score-ids×5."
                (AQP-BOOT.C_Step9_AddFvtScoreEntities p x x x x x ["1" "2" "3" "4" "5" "6"] x x x x))
        ]
    )
    (print "--- [DPDC-G10 · 02 · FIXED: steps 6 and 7 now answer for a SHORT list too] ---")
    ;;Every assertion above passes a list that is too LONG. A too-SHORT list -- the far likelier
    ;;operator mistake, since these are hand-assembled id lists -- used to get no message at all.
    ;;
    ;;Both functions bound the list elements ABOVE their own enforce, in an eager `let`:
    ;;    Step 6  (silver-boost-class-id (at 0 boost-class-ids)) … (at 2 …)
    ;;            (enforce (= (length boost-class-ids) 3) "Step 6 expects …")
    ;;    Step 7  (at 0 … at 8 dh-score-ids), then four enforces below them
    ;;so Pact evaluated `(at 2 …)` / `(at 8 …)` first and raised a NATIVE index fault:
    ;;    Array index out of bounds. Length (2), Index (2)
    ;;instead of the sentence naming the ids to pass. And an index fault is not recoverable --
    ;;`enforce-one` cannot catch it and neither can `try` (both established earlier in this suite) --
    ;;so no wrapper could have softened it either. **Six operator-facing messages, reachable only for
    ;;the input an operator is least likely to produce.**
    ;;
    ;;FIXED 2026-09-12, owner-ruled: the five length enforces are hoisted above their `let`s. Length
    ;;tests need nothing but the parameters, so they run before anything is derived -- the same shape
    ;;used for 21_CODEX:332, and the cheapest repair in this class.
    (map print
        [
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G10>> step 6 with too FEW ids now names the argument"
                "Step 6 expects boost-class-ids=[silver bronze golden]."
                (AQP-BOOT.C_Step6_CreateOuroLpTriplet p p x ["a" "b"]))
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G10>> ...and so does step 7, on its nine-score list"
                "Step 7 expects dh-score-ids="
                (AQP-BOOT.C_Step7_CreatePoolsAndScores p a6 x ["1" "2"] ["s" "b" "g"]))
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G10>> ...and on its asset list, which is a DIFFERENT message"
                "Step 7 expects dh-asset-ids="
                (AQP-BOOT.C_Step7_CreatePoolsAndScores p ["a"] x a9 ["s" "b" "g"]))
            ;;THE SAME INPUTS, TOO LONG, still reach the same messages -- section 01 above drives
            ;;those. So the hoist made the short case speak without changing the long one, which is
            ;;the property that makes it a repair rather than a rewrite.
            ;;STEP 9 IS THE CORRECTLY-ORDERED TWIN, and it is what makes the three above a defect
            ;;rather than a Pact limitation. Its enforce sits BEFORE any `at`, so the SAME too-short
            ;;input gets the written message. Same file, same author, same kind of check -- so the fix
            ;;is already demonstrated in place, and nothing about step 6/7 forces their ordering.
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G10>> ...while step 9, which enforces FIRST, speaks for a short list too"
                "Step 9 expects subsidiary-score-ids×5."
                (AQP-BOOT.C_Step9_AddFvtScoreEntities p x x x x x ["1" "2"] x x x x))
        ]
    )
    ""
)
(commit-tx)

