(begin-tx "DPDC-G11 — add-quantity's input domain, and a regression guard on a past hoist fix")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 150000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== DPDC-G11 — add-quantity's input domain, and a regression guard on a past hoist fix · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== 
(print "--- [DPDC-G11 — add-quantity's input domain, and a regression guard on a past hoist fix · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
(print "--- [DPDC-G11 · 01 · nonce and amount domain] ---")
;;DPDC-MNG|C>ADD-QUANTITY carries two enforces that share ONE message, and both sit in front of the
;;ownership and role checks -- so all three inputs below are refused for the DATA, not the caller.
;;
;;THE FIRST ONE IS A REGRESSION GUARD, and the source says why (06_DPDC-MNG.pact:287-292). `(> nonce 0)`
;;used to live INSIDE the let, below `(UR_NonceClass id true nonce)`. That reader hard-reads keyed by
;;the nonce, so **nonce 0 aborted on "row not found" and never reached the guard** -- while a NEGATIVE
;;nonce DID reach it, because `UR_NonceClass` keys on `(abs nonce)` and so found a row that exists.
;;A partially-shadowed guard: mute for one input, live for another. It was hoisted above the let.
;;
;;Nothing was testing that hoist. The first two assertions below are that test: nonce 0 and nonce -1
;;must BOTH answer with the written message. Put the enforce back inside the let and the nonce-0 case
;;starts failing with a row-not-found error instead. This is the same class as the AQP-BOOT steps in
;;DPDC-G10 -- except here the fix is already applied, which is why it is worth protecting.
;;==== DPDC-G11 — add-quantity's input domain, and a regression guard on a past hoist fix · 02 · let / invocation ==== 
(print "--- [DPDC-G11 — add-quantity's input domain, and a regression guard on a past hoist fix · 02 · let / invocation] ---")
(let
    (
        (sf:string "MOCKS-98c486052a51")
        (a:string KST.ANHD)
    )
    (map print
        [
            ;;the fixture the guard is read against: four nonces, all class 0
            (expect "<<DPDC-G11>> the SFT collection has four nonces, all of class zero"
                [4 [0 0 0 0]]
                [(DPDC.UR_NoncesUsed sf true)
                 (map (lambda (n:integer) (DPDC.UR_NonceClass sf true n))
                      (enumerate 1 (DPDC.UR_NoncesUsed sf true)))])
            ;;NONCE 0 -- the case the hoist fixed. Nonce numbering starts at 1, so 0 is "no nonce".
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G11>> nonce 0 is refused with the written message, not a row error"
                "Invalid Data for Adding Quantity for an SFT Nonce"
                (TS02-C1.DPSF|C_AddQuantity a a sf 0 5))
            ;;NONCE -1 -- reached the guard even before the hoist, because UR_NonceClass takes (abs n)
            ;;and therefore read nonce 1's row. Asserted alongside nonce 0 because the two differ in
            ;;exactly the way that made the old bug hard to see: only one of them was mute.
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G11>> ...and so is a negative nonce, the half that always worked"
                "Invalid Data for Adding Quantity for an SFT Nonce"
                (TS02-C1.DPSF|C_AddQuantity a a sf -1 5))
            ;;AMOUNT 0 -- the SECOND enforce, inside the let, sharing the same message. Adding zero
            ;;quantity is a no-op that would still cost IGNIS and emit an event.
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G11>> adding a ZERO quantity is refused as a no-op"
                "Invalid Data for Adding Quantity for an SFT Nonce"
                (TS02-C1.DPSF|C_AddQuantity a a sf 1 0))
            ;;...and the positive direction, so none of the above is satisfied by a path that refuses
            ;;everything. A real nonce with a real amount is accepted.
            (expect "<<DPDC-G11>> ...while a real nonce with a positive amount is accepted"
                true (!= "" (TS02-C1.DPSF|C_AddQuantity a a sf 1 5)))
        ]
    )
    ""
)
;;the accepted add-quantity above changed a nonce's supply
(rollback-tx)

;;|| NEXT >
