(begin-tx "DPDC-G11b — the add-quantity fixture left no trace")
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [DPDC-G11b · 01 · no leakage] ---")
;;DPDC-G11 added 5 to nonce 1's supply to prove the guards are a domain check and not a blanket
;;refusal. Rolled back, because later assertions in this file and in DPDC-FRAGMENTS read these
;;supplies.
;;
;;NOT PINNED HERE, with the reason: DPDC-MNG|C>IZ-CLASS-ZERO's "Invalid Class Zero Nonces" needs a
;;nonce whose class is NOT zero, and every nonce in this fixture is class 0 (asserted in DPDC-G11).
;;Non-zero classes are created by SET definition, which lives in DPDC-S -- so that guard belongs in
;;`modules/DPDC-S.repl`, not here. Recorded so the next pass does not re-derive it.
;;THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS ASSERTION WAS VACUOUS, and worth leaving a note about because of how it
;;happened. I wrote `(= (UR_NonceSupply …) (UR_NonceSupply …))` -- the same expression twice -- which
;;is always true. A leakage check degrades into that shape naturally: the two things you want to
;;compare are "the value now" and "the value before", and with no handle on "before" it is one
;;keystroke to write "now" twice. `_vacuous.py` reported a clean 0, because its VACUOUS test compared
;;the EXPECTED against the ACTUAL and was blind to a self-comparison nested INSIDE the actual. The tool
;;now has a `self_comparison` check (plus two synthetic selftest cases) and names this exact site.
;;
;;The fix is to assert against the literal value that is actually there. Note it is 6, NOT the 10 that
;;DPDC-02 pins at the top of this file: the transactions in between (the multi-transfer and the burn
;;paths) moved it. Getting that wrong is how the corrected assertion first failed -- which is the
;;point, because a real value is something the test can be wrong about, and a self-comparison is not.
(print
    (expect "<<DPDC-G11b>> nonce 1's supply is back to 6, so the accepted +5 was rolled back"
        6 (DPDC.UR_NonceSupply "MOCKS-98c486052a51" true 1)))
(commit-tx)

