(begin-tx "DPDC-G14 — FIXED: the bulk-transfer shape guard now answers in all three directions")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 150000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== DPDC-G14 — FIXED: the bulk-transfer shape guard now answers in all three directions · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== 
(print "--- [DPDC-G14 — FIXED: the bulk-transfer shape guard now answers in all three directions · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
(print "--- [DPDC-G14 · 01 · which mismatches reach the message, and which do not] ---")
;;`DPDC-T|C>BULK-TRANSFER` folds three shape conditions under one message:
;;    (> l 0)   (= l (length nonces-array))   (= l (length amounts-array))
;;and the cap itself is written correctly -- nothing indexes before the enforce. The guard is
;;nonetheless UNREACHABLE for two of the three ways to break it, and the reason is in the TALOS
;;WRAPPER, not the core.
;;
;;`TS02-C1::DPDC|C_BulkTransfer` derives two helper lists in an EAGER `let`, BEFORE calling the core:
;;    (l:integer (length receiver-lst))
;;    (ids:[string]  (map (lambda (idx) id)  (enumerate 0 (- l 1))))
;;    (sons:[bool]   (map (lambda (idx) son) (enumerate 0 (- l 1))))
;;    (irs: … (ref-DPDC-T::C_IgnisRoyaltyCollector patron sender ids sons nonces-array amounts-array))
;;
;;`(enumerate 0 (- l 1))` is the hazard. For l = 0 it is `(enumerate 0 -1)`, which in Pact is
;;**[0, -1] -- a DESCENDING two-element range, not an empty list** (asserted below, and recorded in
;;2026-09-11-enumerate-counts-down.md). So an EMPTY receiver list produces TWO ids and TWO sons, the
;;royalty collector indexes past the end of the one-element arrays, and the caller gets
;;`Array index out of bounds` instead of the sentence written for exactly this mistake.
;;
;;THE SAME FAULT, ONE DIRECTION ONLY. When the receiver list is LONGER than the nonce legs the derived
;;lists are again longer than the arrays, so that mismatch is mute too. Only the opposite direction --
;;arrays longer than receivers - survives to the enforce, because then the indexing stays in bounds.
;;
;;THE FIX WAS IN THE REPO, one module over. `TS01-C1::DPOF|C_BulkTransfer` uses the IDENTICAL idiom
;;and was NEVER mute: it calls the core FIRST and derives AFTERWARDS, so the guard answers before the
;;hazardous expression is evaluated. The bug was purely the ORDER.
;;(DPOF's working behaviour is pinned by DPOF-G12, which gets the message for an empty receiver list.)
;;
;;FIXED 2026-09-12, owner-ruled: the core `C_BulkTransfer` call is hoisted above the royalty
;;collector, matching DPOF. Royalties are computed from the collectable's creator settings and the
;;amounts -- not from balances -- so moving the call does not change what is charged.
;;
;;TWO enumerate hazards, not one, and that is why the ORDER fix was the right one rather than
;;repairing the expression: the Talos wrapper had `(enumerate 0 (- l 1))` AND
;;`C_IgnisRoyaltyCollector` has its own `(enumerate 0 (- (length ids) 1))` indexing
;;`(at idx nonces-array)`. A first attempt rewrote only the wrapper's -- mapping over `receiver-lst`
;;itself, which is genuinely empty for an empty list -- and the second hazard still faulted. That
;;rewrite was kept anyway (it removes a latent trap and is identical for l>0), but hoisting the core
;;is what makes ALL downstream enumerates operate on lists already proven to agree.
;;==== DPDC-G14 — FIXED: the bulk-transfer shape guard now answers in all three directions · 02 · let / invocation ==== 
(print "--- [DPDC-G14 — FIXED: the bulk-transfer shape guard now answers in all three directions · 02 · let / invocation] ---")
(let
    (
        (ref-TS02-C2:module{TalosStageTwo_ClientTwoV2} TS02-C2)
        (nf:string "MOCKN-98c486052a51")
    )
    (map print
        [
            ;;the root cause, asserted on its own so the finding does not rest on reading Pact docs
            (expect "<<DPDC-G14>> (enumerate 0 -1) is a DESCENDING PAIR, not an empty list"
                [0 -1] (enumerate 0 -1))
            (expect "<<DPDC-G14>> ...whereas a one-element range is what the code assumes it gets"
                [0] (enumerate 0 0))
            ;;WAS MUTE 1 -- an empty receiver list. `(> l 0)` exists for precisely this and used to
            ;;never run. It runs now.
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G14>> an EMPTY receiver list reaches the shape message"
                "Invalid DPDC bulk transfer: receiver/nonces/amounts legs"
                (ref-TS02-C2::DPNF|C_BulkTransfer KST.ANHD KST.ANHD [] nf [[1]] [[1]] false))
            ;;WAS MUTE 2 -- more receivers than nonce legs. Same cause, same repair.
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G14>> ...and so does two receivers against one nonce leg"
                "Invalid DPDC bulk transfer: receiver/nonces/amounts legs"
                (ref-TS02-C2::DPNF|C_BulkTransfer KST.ANHD KST.ANHD [KST.EMMA KST.LUMY] nf [[1]] [[1]] false))
            ;;ALWAYS REACHABLE -- arrays longer than receivers. The indexing stayed in bounds even
            ;;before the fix, so this direction always spoke. Keeping it is what shows the repair
            ;;made the guard UNIFORM rather than simply moving which direction works.
            (expect-failure "<<DPDC-G14>> ...while two nonce legs against one receiver DOES reach the message"
                "Invalid DPDC bulk transfer: receiver/nonces/amounts legs"
                (ref-TS02-C2::DPNF|C_BulkTransfer KST.ANHD KST.ANHD [KST.EMMA] nf [[1] [2]] [[1]] false))
        ]
    )
    ""
)
(env-sigs [])
(commit-tx)

;;|| NEXT >
